Input: {"question": "What political party does Marcoux belong to?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 0, "end": 74, "text": "in l'Association des Commissaires d'écoles Canadiens-Français du Manitoba."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 75, "end": 138, "text": "He was also a member of the Laurier Club and the Canadian Club."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 139, "end": 271, "text": "He was a Liberal by background, and became a member of the Liberal-Progressives following the merger of the two constituent parties."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 272, "end": 315, "text": "In 1920, Marcoux married Eloria Normandeau."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 316, "end": 492, "text": "He was first elected to the Manitoba legislature in the 1936 provincial election, defeating Conservative candidate Joseph Hamelin by 830 votes in the La Verendrye constituency."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 493, "end": 649, "text": "He was appointed to Bracken's cabinet on September 27, 1936 as a minister without portfolio, representing the interests of Manitoba's francophone community."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d00", "start": 650, "end": 675, "text": "Marcoux was re-elected in"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d01", "start": 0, "end": 38, "text": ", Stuart Garson and Douglas Campbell ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae49f5:d01", "start": 39, "end": 163, "text": "[ 1 ] The son of Leon Marcoux and Adeline Ferland , Marcoux was born in Lorette , Manitoba , was educated in Lorette and St."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0128-30efe7ae